## Summary

The locality derivation now prefers the postcode, falling back to the city.

**`PostcodeRange.java`** — added `regionForPostcode(String)`, the reverse of the region-to-range table it already owns. It returns the region whose inclusive range contains the postcode, or `null` when the postcode is absent, non-numeric, or in no known range. This keeps the range knowledge in the one class that already holds it.

**`OwnerMapper.locality(Owner)`** — the DTO derivation now coordinates the two lookups: postcode first, then the existing `CityRegion` city table as fallback:

```java
default String locality(Owner owner) {
    String byPostcode = PostcodeRange.regionForPostcode(owner.getPostcode());
    return byPostcode != null ? byPostcode : CityRegion.locality(owner.getCity());
}
```

Known cities still resolve to the same region (their pinned postcodes fall in-range), while a postcode disambiguates cities sharing a name — e.g. an unknown city with a `3000` postcode now yields `VIC`. No logic was copied: `CityRegion` still owns the city table, `PostcodeRange` owns the ranges, and the mapper method remains the small single-responsibility derivation unit.

